-- title:
--   Finiteness of the kernel of a push–pull operator on M× M
-- statement:
--   Let $M$ be an additive commutative group, $p$ a natural number, and $F,V,\delta,\delta'\colon M\to M$ additive endomorphisms subject to: $F\circ V = p\cdot\mathrm{id}_M$ and $V\circ F = p\cdot\mathrm{id}_M$ (the $p$-fold multiple of the identity endomorphism), $\delta\circ\delta' = \mathrm{id}_M$ and $\delta'\circ\delta = \mathrm{id}_M$, and the four commutation relations $F\circ\delta = \delta\circ F$, $V\circ\delta = \delta\circ V$, $F\circ\delta' = \delta'\circ F$, $V\circ\delta' = \delta'\circ V$. Assume moreover that the two sets $\{x \in M : x - \delta(F(F(x))) = 0\}$ and $\{x \in M : x - \delta'(V(V(x))) = 0\}$ are finite, i.e. the endomorphisms $1-\delta F^2$ and $1-\delta' V^2$ have finite kernel. The conclusion is that the set of pairs $(a,b) \in M\times M$ satisfying the two equations
--   $$(1+p)\cdot a + \bigl(V(b) + F(\delta(b))\bigr) = 0, \qquad \bigl(F(a) + \delta'(V(a))\bigr) + (1+p)\cdot b = 0$$
--   is finite; here $(1+p)\cdot$ denotes the natural-number scalar multiple. Thus the kernel of the endomorphism of $M\times M$ given by the matrix with rows $(1+p,\; V+F\delta)$ and $(F+\delta' V,\; 1+p)$ is finite.
--
--   This is the finiteness statement for the kernel of the push–pull (degeneracy) operator attached to a pair of Atkin–Lehner type matrices over the commutative ring generated by Frobenius, Verschiebung and the involution $\delta$; it is stated purely in terms of additive group endomorphisms, with no geometric input. It is used in the analysis of the component groups and degeneracy maps on the Néron model at $p$ of a Jacobian, where it supplies the finiteness needed for the statement [`ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_nsmul_eq_zero_of_forall_degPts_pull_add_pull_eq_zero`](thm.html#ModularCurve.JHNeronObjectAtP.exists_nsmul_eq_zero_of_forall_degPts_pull_add_pull_eq_zero).
